{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "tsBuildInfoFile": "./node_modules/.tmp/tsconfig.node.tsbuildinfo",
    "target": "es2023",
    "lib": ["ES2023"],
    "types": ["node"],
    "skipLibCheck": true,

    /* Bundler mode */
    "module": "nodenext",
    "allowImportingTsExtensions": true,
    "verbatimModuleSyntax": true,
    "moduleDetection": "force",
    "noEmit": true,

    /* Linting */
    "strict": true,
    "noUnusedLocals": true,
    "noUnusedParameters": true,
    "erasableSyntaxOnly": true,
    "noFallthroughCasesInSwitch": true
  },
  /*
    `vitest.config.ts` is typechecked here alongside `vite.config.ts` — both are node-side
    config, neither is application source. Leaving it out would mean `npm run build` never
    looks at it, and a broken test config would only surface when someone ran the tests.

    The contract checks are the rest, and they are odd ones out only by location: they sit in
    `src/` beside the modules they inspect, but they read `openapi.yaml` off disk and drive the
    TypeScript compiler over the tree, so they need `types: ["node"]` — which is exactly
    what `tsconfig.app.json` must not have. See the note there.

    **This list and `tsconfig.app.json`'s exclude list are hand-maintained and must agree.** A file
    added to one and not the other is either typechecked with `node` missing (TS2591 on every
    `node:` import, which is how §196 found out) or not typechecked at all. Nothing enforces the
    pairing — a small instance of exactly what `contractFixtures.test.ts` was written about.
  */
  "include": [
    "vite.config.ts",
    "vitest.config.ts",
    "vitest.smoke.config.ts",
    "src/api/contractParameters.test.ts",
    "src/api/apiResponseTypes.test.ts",
    "src/api/contractOperations.test.ts",
    "src/api/contractFixtures.test.ts",
    "src/api/contractIntrospection.ts",
    "src/app/tokenSystem.test.ts",
    "src/app/statusPalette.test.ts",
    "src/app/noVelocity.test.ts",
    "src/app/projectPalette.test.ts"
  ]
}
